@prefix cell: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix p: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/persona#> .
@prefix o: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/organization#> .
@prefix agent: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/agent#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix dc: <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/> .
@base <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell> .

<http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell/3.47.0> ;
    dc:date "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Cell Ontology"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Cell Ontology for the Cellula App. A cell is the atomic unit of the user's tree: a filesystem folder holding exactly one cell-databook file, folder and file together forming one tree node. Defines cell:Cell — the self-contained content facet of a cell, carried by that cell-databook file's own triples. cell:Cell stores no property recording the cell's own tree position: that position is simply whichever folder the cell-databook currently lives in, not a fact this RDF individual needs to carry. cell:Cell splits into two disjoint kinds: cell:TemplateCell (a reusable, class-level template) and cell:MemberCell (an actual cell instantiated in a user's own tree) — a cell is always exactly one of the two, never both. cell:MemberCell further specializes into cell:TopicCell, for a member cell that actually carries a cell:topic value beyond its cell:member baseline. Also defines cell:Graph and cell:SCGraph — the graph-DataBook classes cell:member/cell:topic link to — and their cell:template/cell:subject/cell:claimant annotation properties, since a graph DataBook only ever exists to be linked from a cell."""@en .


#################################################################
#    Annotation Properties for Cell DataBook Classification
#################################################################

cell:abstract rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:domain owl:Class ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "abstract"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "True if this class is not directly instantiated in DataBooks — it exists only to organize the class hierarchy."@en .

#################################################################
#    Cell
#
#    A cell is the atomic unit of the user's tree: a filesystem folder
#    holding exactly one cell-databook file, folder and file together
#    forming one tree node — cells nest inside cells, forming the tree.
#    cell:Cell is the self-contained content record carried by that
#    cell-databook file's own triples. It stores no property recording
#    the cell's own tree position: that position is simply whichever
#    folder the cell-databook currently occupies, not a fact this
#    individual needs to carry — moving the folder is a pure
#    filesystem operation, asserting nothing new on the cell itself.
#    The folder<->cell-databook relationship is strictly one-to-one,
#    not many-to-one: a folder holds at most one cell-databook file,
#    whose filename must be an exact copy of the folder's own name —
#    i.e. a folder either IS a cell (it holds exactly one
#    cell-databook) or it is a plain filesystem folder that is not a
#    cell at all, possibly with nested cells of its own further down.
#    There is no way for two cell:Cell individuals to share one
#    folder: a cell is self-contained, and letting two cells share a
#    folder would risk a single file in that folder becoming
#    ambiguously part of both.
#
#    cell:Cell itself carries only what's common to every cell
#    regardless of facet: inherent classification (cell:category).
#    Everything facet-specific — including cell:member/cell:topic
#    (its linked cell:SCGraph container(s)) — lives on
#    cell:TemplateCell or cell:MemberCell (and, for cell:topic, its
#    cell:TopicCell subclass) below. There is no stored property
#    recording who or what a cell's relationship is about: that fact
#    is derivable directly from cell:member/cell:topic (see their own
#    comments below) rather than asserted independently — a subject
#    property on cell:Cell itself once existed here but was removed
#    once every real example showed its value was always mechanically
#    computable from the cell:subject values of the graphs already
#    linked. That cell:subject property (below) is a different,
#    surviving property, scoped to cell:SCGraph rather than cell:Cell
#    — see the "Cell Topic Links" section further down for how the two
#    relate.
#################################################################

cell:Cell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:label "Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Superclass of every cell individual, carrying only what's common regardless of kind: inherent classification (cell:category). cell:Cell — the RDF content facet of a cell — stores no property recording its own tree position: a cell's position is simply whichever folder it (folder + this cell-databook) currently occupies, and moving that folder is a pure filesystem operation requiring no triple to change, which is what keeps a cell robust under PDN sync. cell:category does not violate this: its range is skos:Concept — a category concept scoped to cat:CategoryScheme (category.ttl), not a tree position — so it records what kind of thing the cell is, never where it currently lives. Creator and ownership metadata (cell:creator, cell:owner) and graph links (cell:member, cell:topic) live on the cell:MemberCell kind (and, for cell:topic, its cell:TopicCell subclass), not directly here; validation shape is not stored on either kind, only derived (see cell:MemberCell's own comment)."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Kinds: Template (TemplateCell) and Member (MemberCell)
#
#    A cell:Cell splits into two disjoint kinds — a cell is always
#    exactly one, never both:
#
#    cell:TemplateCell — a reusable, class-level template. Carries
#    cell:category, naming the category concept (a skos:Concept in
#    cat:CategoryScheme, category.ttl) it's a template for, and
#    cell:templateShape, describing what a graph filed under that
#    category is expected to look like once instantiated. Finding the
#    template for a given category is a reverse lookup (?tc cell:category
#    cat:X) rather than a forward property chain — category.ttl no
#    longer points at cell-templates.ttl at all. Every cell:TemplateCell
#    individual lives in cell-templates.ttl, never in a user's own tree.
#
#    cell:MemberCell — an actual cell instantiated in a user's own
#    tree. Carries cell:creator, cell:owner (one or more of the cell's
#    members holding the owner role — see cell:owner's own comment),
#    and cell:member, its required baseline of linked cell:SCGraph
#    containers, one per member in the relationship (no fixed upper
#    bound). There is no stored cell:shape any more —
#    a MemberCell's own validation expectation is derivable by the same
#    reverse cell:category lookup that finds its TemplateCell, then
#    reading that TemplateCell's cell:templateShape directly, so storing
#    a second copy on the MemberCell itself was pure redundancy (the
#    same reasoning that already removed cell:Cell's own subject
#    property — not to be confused with cell:subject, the surviving
#    annotation property scoped to cell:SCGraph, below). A cell:MemberCell
#    that also
#    carries one or more cell:topic values — additional
#    cell:SCGraph containers beyond that baseline — is additionally typed
#    cell:TopicCell (see below), the only class cell:topic's domain
#    admits.
#
#    cell:TemplateCell and cell:MemberCell are owl:disjointWith one
#    another: every individual in cell-templates.ttl is typed
#    cell:TemplateCell only,
#    carrying cell:templateShape and nothing from cell:MemberCell's
#    side — it is reusable template content, not itself a piece of
#    member-classified content. When Lazy Instantiation clones that
#    template into a new cell for a user's tree, the clone is a wholly
#    separate individual, typed cell:MemberCell (and cell:TopicCell
#    once/if it later gains a cell:topic value) instead.
#################################################################

cell:TemplateCell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:Cell ;
    owl:disjointWith cell:MemberCell ;
    rdfs:label "Template Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A reusable, class-level cell template — one of the two disjoint kinds of cell:Cell, the other being cell:MemberCell. A cell:TemplateCell individual carries its own cell:category value naming the category concept (a skos:Concept in cat:CategoryScheme, category.ttl) it's a template for — it's what the app clones into a new cell when a category of that concept is first instantiated into a user's tree (Lazy Instantiation). Finding the template for a given category concept is a reverse lookup (?tc cell:category cat:X), since category.ttl carries no forward pointer of its own any more. Carries cell:templateShape, linking to the sh:NodeShape(s) describing the content a graph filed under that category is expected to conform to, and cell:isTopicCell, flagging whether the clone should be given the cell:TopicCell mixin. Most cell:TemplateCell individuals carry no cell:templateShape value — that's the default, not an omission. Disjoint with cell:MemberCell: a cell:TemplateCell individual is never also typed cell:MemberCell or any of its subclasses (including cell:TopicCell) — see cell-templates.ttl, where every template individual is typed solely cell:Cell, cell:TemplateCell."@en .

cell:templateShape rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:TemplateCell ;
    rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "templateShape"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a cell:TemplateCell individual directly to the sh:NodeShape(s) that describe the content a graph filed under that individual's own cell:category concept is expected to conform to — e.g. ctpl:PassportTemplateCell (cell-templates.ttl) carries cell:category cat:Passport and cell:templateShape pshapes:PassportDocumentShape (cell-templates-shacl.ttl). A template cell with no known shape (most of them) carries no cell:templateShape value. A real cell:MemberCell cloned from this template does not carry its own copy of this value — there is no cell:shape property — since the same shape is always reachable on demand from the MemberCell's own cell:category value via this same reverse lookup (?tc cell:category cat:X ; cell:templateShape ?shape), making a stored per-instance copy pure redundancy."@en .

cell:isTopicCell rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:TemplateCell ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "isTopicCell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A boolean flag on a cell:TemplateCell individual telling Lazy Instantiation whether the cell it clones into a user's tree is expected to be typed cell:TopicCell — i.e. to carry a cell:topic value once real content is filed under it — as opposed to staying a bare cell:MemberCell with no cell:topic. Required, exactly one value — every cell:TemplateCell individual must declare it explicitly, one way or the other, rather than leaving it to an absent-means-false default. E.g. ctpl:MedicalAppointmentTemplateCell and ctpl:PetMedicationsTemplateCell (cell-templates.ttl) carry cell:isTopicCell true, since a real Medical Appointment or Medications cell always ends up about a third party (Paula, Ginger) beyond its own cell:member baseline; ctpl:PassportTemplateCell/ctpl:BirthCertificateTemplateCell/ctpl:DriversLicenseTemplateCell carry cell:isTopicCell false, since a real cell cloned from any of them never gains a cell:topic value. Does not itself assert cell:TopicCell on the template individual — cell:TemplateCell and cell:MemberCell (and its cell:TopicCell subclass) are disjoint; this flag only describes what the eventual clone should be typed as, once instantiated."@en .

cell:MemberCell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:label "Member Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An actual cell instantiated in a user's own tree — real, member-classified content, as opposed to a reusable class-level template (cell:TemplateCell). Carries cell:creator (required, exactly one value), cell:owner (required, one or more values — the members holding the owner role, as opposed to the regular member role every other member defaults to; cell:creator's own value is always included, as the cell's initial owner — see cell:owner's own comment for the full owner/regular-member role semantics), and cell:member (required, one or more cell:SCGraph containers backing the cell's content — one per member in the relationship, no fixed upper bound). There is no cell:shape property: a cell:MemberCell's applicable validation shape is not stored at all, only derived, via its own cell:category value — find the cell:TemplateCell with the same cell:category (cell-templates.ttl) and read that individual's cell:templateShape directly. A cell:MemberCell that also carries one or more cell:topic values is additionally typed cell:TopicCell, the only class cell:topic (one or more additional cell:SCGraph containers beyond the cell:member baseline) has domain over. Who or what the cell's relationship is about is not a stored fact either — it is derivable directly from cell:member/cell:topic: the full set of distinct subjects among cell:topic if any are linked, else the full set of distinct subjects among cell:member (see cell:topic's own comment). Every real cell:Cell individual in a user's own tree is typed directly cell:MemberCell — its member count is never itself stored as a class, simply counted from cell:member/cell:topic when needed. Disjoint with cell:TemplateCell (see cell:TemplateCell's own comment): every cell:Cell individual in a user's own tree is typed cell:MemberCell, never cell:TemplateCell, which is reserved for the reusable templates in cell-templates.ttl. Even a purely organizational cell with nothing substantive to say carries a minimal stub cell:member entry (claimed by and about cell:creator's own :Self, who is then also that cell's sole cell:owner value) rather than omitting member content altogether — such a cell has no third party to link via cell:topic, so it stays a bare cell:MemberCell, not also cell:TopicCell."@en .

cell:creator rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:MemberCell ;
    rdfs:range [ rdf:type owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( p:Person o:Organization ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "creator"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identifies who created a cell's content — a named individual of p:Person or o:Organization. Required, exactly one value, since every actual cell's content is authored by someone. Mirrors the union-range pattern used by cell:claimant (below), referencing p:Person/o:Organization by name without owl:imports — though cell:claimant's own range is wider, also admitting agent:Agent. Only actual/instantiated cells (cell:MemberCell) have a creator."@en .

cell:owner rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:MemberCell ;
    rdfs:range [ rdf:type owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( p:Person o:Organization ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "owner"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identifies which of a cell:MemberCell's members hold the owner role, as opposed to the regular (non-owner) member role every other member defaults to — a named individual of p:Person or o:Organization, the same narrower union cell:creator's own range uses (never agent:Agent — an invited agent:Agent can join a cell as a member but can never be promoted to owner, mirroring cell:member's own comment that an agent:Agent can join a cell as a member but never creates one). Required, one or more values (sh:minCount 1, no maxCount, enforced by cell-shacl.ttl's :MemberCellShape) — every cell:MemberCell has at least one owner and may have several, since ownership is additive only: cell:creator's own value is always included among cell:owner's values as the cell's initial (and, until any promotion, sole) owner, and any current owner may add any other current regular member (identified by cell:member, not cell:topic) to cell:owner, promoting them — there is no mechanism modeled here for removing a value from cell:owner once added, i.e. no demotion. An owner has full read/write access to the cell's note (app-level; see APP-BEHAVIOR.md's Permissions section) and may delete any member's claim or attachment, not only their own; a regular (non-owner) member may only comment on or suggest edits to the note, and may only create/read/update/delete their own claims. Every cell:owner value is also one of the cell's active members — i.e. it must equal the cell:subject of at least one of that cell's cell:member graphs — an invariant not itself OWL/SHACL-expressible (it requires dereferencing each linked graph's own subject, not just counting or matching cardinalities), so it is checked only by CLAUDE.md's own integrity check (Check 23), not by cell-shacl.ttl."@en .

#################################################################
#    Topic Cell
#
#    cell:TopicCell is a subclass of cell:MemberCell for a member cell
#    that actually carries one or more cell:topic values — additional
#    cell:SCGraph containers beyond its cell:member baseline,
#    typically about a third party (or parties) the cell's
#    relationship concerns but who aren't among its members (e.g.
#    Paula Walker, linked via cell:topic from Alice's "Paula Walker"
#    employee cell, whose cell:member baseline is just Alice's own
#    graph). Most cells have no cell:topic value at all —
#    cell:TopicCell is the minority subclass, not the default. Not
#    itself abstract: a real cell:MemberCell with a cell:topic value
#    is directly instantiated as a cell:TopicCell too.
#################################################################

cell:TopicCell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:MemberCell ;
    rdfs:label "Topic Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A cell:MemberCell that actually carries at least one cell:topic value — its rdfs:domain. Carries cell:topic, one or more additional cell:SCGraph containers beyond the cell:member baseline; cell:member itself stays on cell:MemberCell (every member cell carries it, required), but cell:topic is scoped to cell:TopicCell specifically, since an 'other' graph (or graphs) about a third party is an additional fact some — not all — member cells carry. A cell:MemberCell with no cell:topic value (the common case) stays a bare cell:MemberCell, never also cell:TopicCell — see CLAUDE.md's TopicCell integrity check for the mechanical rule (non-empty cell:topic implies rdf:type cell:TopicCell, and vice versa)."@en .


#################################################################
#    Annotation Properties for Graph DataBook Classification
#
#    A graph DataBook only ever exists to be linked from a cell (via
#    cell:member/cell:topic below), so its own classification
#    properties live here in cell.ttl rather than in a separate
#    ontology/file.
#################################################################

cell:claimant rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "claimed by"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Annotates a graph DataBook with the identity making the claim —
                    a named individual of p:Person, o:Organization, or agent:Agent
                    (e.g. :Self, a p:Person or o:Organization instance; or an invited
                    agent:Agent, self-claiming its own cell:member entry and claiming
                    whatever cell:topic content it contributes — see agent.ttl). Applied
                    to the graph DataBook's own IRI (its plain `id`) — the same IRI
                    cell:member/cell:topic link to from the cell side —
                    not to the `#graph`-suffixed `graph.named_graph` IRI, and not
                    to the Person/Organization/Agent individual."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain cell:SCGraph ;
    rdfs:range [ rdf:type owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( p:Person o:Organization agent:Agent ) ] .

cell:subject rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "subject"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Annotates a graph DataBook with the resource it is about. Value
                    may be any resource IRI — a graph need not be about a person's
                    identity. In the example data, every subject value happens to be a
                    named individual of p:Person or o:Organization (e.g.
                    :Self, a p:Person or o:Organization instance), but that is
                    a convention of the example data, not an ontology requirement.
                    Applied to the graph DataBook's own IRI (its plain `id`) — the same
                    IRI cell:member/cell:topic link to from the cell
                    side — not to the `#graph`-suffixed `graph.named_graph` IRI,
                    and not to the subject resource itself. Not to be confused with a
                    cell's own (derived, never-stored) subject — see cell:Cell's own
                    comment and the "Cell Topic Links" section below."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain cell:SCGraph ;
    rdfs:range xsd:anyURI .

cell:template rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "template"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Annotates a graph DataBook with the persona template type that applies to it.
                    Value is one of the persona:PersonaTemplate subclasses (persona:BirthCertificateDocument,
                    persona:JSContactCard, persona:DriversLicenseDocument, persona:PassportDocument,
                    persona:MedicalAppointmentRecord).
                    Used to select the appropriate per-template SHACL shapes file for validation.
                    Applied to the graph DataBook's own IRI (its plain `id`), not the
                    `#graph`-suffixed `graph.named_graph` IRI — matching cell:subject and
                    cell:claimant."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain cell:Graph ;
    rdfs:range <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/persona#PersonaTemplate> .


#################################################################
#    Graph (DataBook) and SCGraph
#################################################################

cell:Graph rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:label "Graph"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A graph DataBook — a named graph of claims about some resource, filed in a specific relationship or situation, extracted into a named graph (graph.named_graph, the DataBook's own `id` plus a `#graph` fragment) — since this class's instances represent exactly the content that becomes that named graph, see the '#### Graph' DataBook section heading convention. The resource it is about need not be a person's identity — see cell:subject. Instances of this class and its annotations (cell:subject, cell:claimant, cell:template) are typed and asserted on the DataBook's own `id` IRI, not the `#graph`-suffixed named graph IRI that wraps its extracted content."@en .

cell:SCGraph rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "SCGraph"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Subject-Claimant graph: the concrete class every self-vs-other classified graph DataBook is typed as directly — it has no subclasses; the four self-vs-other cases (self-by-self, other-by-self, other-by-other, self-by-other) are determined directly from the combination of subject and claimant. Carries the subject and claimant annotations — every graph reachable from a cell, via cell:member/cell:topic, is a cell:SCGraph and so always carries them."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Topic Links
#
#    cell:member has domain cell:MemberCell; cell:topic has domain
#    cell:TopicCell, the subclass of cell:MemberCell for cells that
#    actually carry a cell:topic value. A cell not yet typed
#    cell:MemberCell (no real content filed under it yet) carries
#    neither; a cell:MemberCell with no cell:topic value stays a bare
#    cell:MemberCell, never gaining cell:TopicCell.
#
#    cell:member is the required baseline — at least one
#    cell:SCGraph per member in the relationship, no fixed upper bound
#    (enforced by cell-shacl.ttl's :MemberCellShape); cell:topic is
#    one or more additional graphs beyond that baseline (required, at
#    least one, once a cell is typed cell:TopicCell at all — no upper
#    bound), present on the minority of cells that actually link a
#    graph about a third party beyond their own members.
#
#    Together these two properties are also how a consumer derives
#    who or what a cell's relationship is about, now that there is no
#    separate stored fact for it: if the cell has any cell:topic
#    values (i.e. it's typed cell:TopicCell), the full set of distinct
#    cell:subject values among them is the answer; otherwise the
#    answer is the full set of distinct cell:subject values found
#    among cell:member (for a single-member cell this is trivially
#    :Self; for a cell with more members and no cell:topic, it is
#    every one of that cell's active members). See
#    README.md's Cell Ontology section for the full rule and worked
#    examples.
#################################################################

cell:member rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:MemberCell ;
    rdfs:range cell:SCGraph ;
    rdfs:label "member"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an actual (instantiated) cell to its required baseline of Subject-Claimant classified graph DataBooks (cell:SCGraph, which has no subclasses) — at least one per member in the relationship, no fixed upper bound (enforced by cell-shacl.ttl's :MemberCellShape) — a cell is not expected to ever have more than a couple dozen members, but nothing here caps it. Which self-vs-other case a given value represents comes from that graph's own subject/claimant combination, not from which property linked it or from a distinct rdf:type. Unlike cell:topic, cell:member carries no bearing on whether a cell is typed cell:TopicCell — that turns solely on cell:topic's presence (see cell:TopicCell's own comment). Every cell:member graph's subject is a real relationship participant — a p:Person, o:Organization, or agent:Agent (e.g. a large Affiliations cell's members can include the affiliated o:Organization itself, not just p:Person individuals; an invited agent:Agent, once a member creator invites their own AI agent into the cell, gets its own self-claimed cell:member entry alongside the human members) — so cell:member content never reaches into an other/*.ttl domain ontology (e.g. pets:Pet); only cell:topic can (see cell:topic's own comment). Unlike cell:member, cell:creator's and cell:owner's own range both stay the narrower p:Person/o:Organization union — an agent:Agent can join a cell as a member but never creates one and can never be promoted to owner (see cell:owner's own comment). Together with cell:topic, this is also the sole basis for deriving who or what the cell's relationship is about — see cell:topic's own comment for the rule."@en .

cell:topic rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:TopicCell ;
    rdfs:range cell:SCGraph ;
    rdfs:label "topic"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a cell:TopicCell individual to one or more additional Subject-Claimant classified graph DataBooks (cell:SCGraph) beyond the required cell:member baseline — typically a third party (or parties) the cell's relationship concerns but who aren't among its members. Required, at least one value, no upper bound — enforced by cell-shacl.ttl's :TopicCellShape (sh:class cell:SCGraph, sh:minCount 1) — a cell is typed cell:TopicCell precisely because it carries at least one such value, not the other way around. Unlike cell:member, whose subject is always a real relationship participant (a p:Person, o:Organization, or agent:Agent — see cell:member's own comment), a cell:topic's subject can be a non-member p:Person or o:Organization (e.g. a family member's primary care physician, still no other/*.ttl ontology involved) or an individual from one of the other/*.ttl domain ontologies for something a person merely has rather than is (e.g. pets:Pet, other/pets.ttl) — other/*.ttl content can only ever reach a cell this way, never via cell:member. When any cell:topic values are present, the full set of distinct cell:subject values among them is who or what the cell is about, distinct from the cell's active members; when absent (the common case — most cells are never typed cell:TopicCell), the cell's subject is simply the full set of distinct cell:subject values among cell:member — i.e. its active members themselves (e.g. a two-member cell with no cell:topic is understood as being about the connection between its two members). There is no independently-asserted subject property on cell:Cell/cell:MemberCell itself — a cell's own subject is always computed this way, from its linked graphs' own cell:subject values, never stored directly on the cell."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Folders and Content (the Attachments Tab)
#
#    A cell IS its folder — the folder and its one cell-databook file
#    together are the cell, not two separately-associated things.
#    Since a folder holds at most one cell-databook (see the Cell
#    section above), this folder<->cell-databook mapping is always
#    unambiguous, with nothing left to store, detect divergence in, or
#    keep in sync. cell:Cell itself still stores no path back to this
#    folder — there is no RDF individual representing the folder as
#    something separate from the cell to point at.
#
#    A cell's attachments, shown in the app's Attachments tab, are
#    the plain files found directly inside its folder EXCEPT:
#      1. the cell-databook file itself; and
#      2. the one file named X.md (matching the folder's own name X),
#         recognized by convention as the folder note for that cell —
#         matching the Obsidian Folder Notes plugin convention, and
#         directly compatible with other PKM folder-note tools such
#         as Logseq or Foam.
#    Attachments are flat, like email attachments: no subfolder is
#    ever counted as one, on disk or in the model. A subfolder found
#    directly inside a cell's own folder is always either a
#    descendant cell (it directly contains its own cell-databook
#    file — a separate, distinct node in the tree of cells, never
#    part of its ancestor's own content; see the cell ownership
#    boundary rule below) or a bare pass-through directory with no
#    cell-databook of its own, existing only to reach a descendant
#    cell nested deeper still (CLAUDE.md's Check 11) — neither kind
#    is ever shown in the Attachments tab.
#    Both (1) and (2) above are naming conventions only, discovered
#    by looking inside the folder — neither is tracked by a separate
#    ontology property. The diagram (images/cell-ontology/cell.png)
#    additionally sketches cell:note, cell:attachment, and cell:chat
#    as arrows off Cell for documentation purposes only — none of the
#    three is an actual property declared here, and none is ever
#    reified as a triple in any real graph (see CLAUDE.md's Check 12
#    and README.md's Documentation-only Properties section).
#
#    Cell ownership boundary: a subfolder is a separate, descendant
#    cell of its own — excluded from its ancestor's Attachments-tab
#    content — if and only if it directly contains a *.databook.md
#    file. Every cell already carries such a file — its own
#    cell-databook, whether real content or an empty placeholder —
#    serving double duty as both content holder and tree-node marker;
#    no other marker is needed. This makes ownership resolvable one
#    folder at a time — check what's directly inside a subfolder, not
#    the whole tree of cells — and robust to the user moving folders
#    around outside the app, since the marker file travels with
#    whatever folder it's inside; there is no separately recorded
#    path to reconcile against.
#
#    Because a cell's own note and its other files share one folder,
#    the single Cells root can be opened directly as a PKM vault
#    (e.g. an Obsidian vault) without hiding anything from it: modern
#    PKM tools handle non-Markdown files natively, so there is no need
#    for a second, notes-only hierarchy kept separate from arbitrary
#    files.
#
#    A folder note's Markdown content may link to any other note
#    anywhere in the user's own tree of cells, not just within its own
#    cell's folder — the same vault-wide linking a PKM tool like
#    Obsidian supports. When a cell is shared, the note's own text
#    (including any links it contains) travels with it like any other
#    cell content, but a link's target does not: if that target lies
#    outside this cell's own boundary (i.e. it isn't part of this
#    cell's own Attachments-tab content or its own folder note), the
#    recipient's tree may not contain that other cell at all, or may
#    organize it completely differently, so the link simply won't
#    resolve on their side. This is an accepted limitation of
#    cross-cell linking, not a defect — nothing in this ontology
#    tracks link targets or attempts to keep them resolvable across a
#    share, consistent with the "no ontology property" treatment of
#    the folder note itself above.
#################################################################

cell:category rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "category"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The category concept this cell was originally instantiated as — a genuine skos:Concept individual (e.g. cat:Others, cat:BankingPayments) scoped to cat:CategoryScheme via its own skos:inScheme value (category.ttl), not a string and not a class — category.ttl's tree is a SKOS concept scheme (skos:broader edges), not an OWL class hierarchy, so no class-value punning is involved here (contrast other/vehicles.ttl's vehicles:hasVehicleType, which does pun a real class hierarchy). Domain cell:Cell (not narrowed to cell:MemberCell) deliberately, since cell:category is asserted on both kinds: for one of the templated concepts (cat:Passport/cat:BirthCertificate/cat:DriversLicense/cat:MedicalAppointment/cat:Vehicles/cat:Pets/cat:PetsMedical), the same category concept is asserted both on the cell:TemplateCell that was cloned (cell-templates.ttl) and on the resulting cell:MemberCell clone; for any other cell, it is simply the category the cell was created to represent, asserted directly with no template involved. At most one value (0..1), on any cell:Cell regardless of kind, matching cell.png's category arrow off Cell. Absent exactly when the cell is of the UserDefined category — a category folder the user created without picking any existing category concept, identified precisely by its cell-databook's filename carrying the literal (custom) disambiguator in place of a category-derived <catType> (e.g. Friends(custom).databook.md), not by a vague 'no canonical counterpart' judgment call. This is a different fact from whether the folder's own name matches its category's skos:prefLabel verbatim — see the green ('Predefined')/purple ('UserDefined') folder-name-text convention documented in APP-BEHAVIOR.md and CLAUDE.md's Check 15/10j, which only applies when a category IS present; a UserDefined (no-category) cell's name is trivially always 'UserDefined' text too, since there is no label to possibly match. This value is fixed at the point the cell was created — it is not re-derived from the folder's current name, so it needs no update if the folder is later moved or renamed, keeping a cell:Cell robust under PDN sync exactly as its own class comment already promises (no stored tree-position property). Purpose: when a cell is shared with another PDN member, the recipient's app can use this value, if present, as a hint for which folder in the recipient's own tree the incoming cell should be filed under — a snapshot of the sender's original classification, robust to either side's tree being organized differently. A folder's own filesystem position has no RDF representation at all — UserDefined is a filename/display convention only, never a category concept. Range skos:Concept is referenced by name without owl:imports, the same by-name pattern already used for cat:CategoryScheme/category.ttl's own concepts and mirroring cell:creator's identical pattern for p:Person/o:Organization (persona.ttl/organization.ttl)."@en .
